  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Water Risk Management in the Poop&#xf3; watershed</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project builds on work between University College London (UCL), CAFOD and CENDA is to assess the vulnerability of communities in the Lake Poopo that are affected by contaminated water sources, support communities in monitoring water quality and foster a dialogue on water as a vital resource and as a potential crisis driver in the region. In seeking to develop the dialogue, the research has three specific objectives:
&#x2022;	to survey various types of potential water-related risks and associated crisis drivers that could affect the region; 
&#x2022;	to promote new types of alliances, such as practitioner/ and policy-maker/scientist dialogues, for addressing the threats that the region may face;
&#x2022;	to propose steps that must be taken now to lead to prevention and preparedness measures commensurate with the nature and scale of the potential water risk facing the region.</narrative>
  </description>
